## Environment constraints

- **No `python`, `pip`, `curl`, `wget`, or `nc`** in this container, so you
  cannot execute Python snippets here. Note that `node` *is* present and has a
  global `fetch`, so HTTP is technically available to you — the boundary is
  enforced by network topology, not by the absence of a client. Reaching for it
  to get at data is out of bounds regardless of whether it would work.
- **No access to the data-side filesystem.** You cannot read raw data, list its
  directories, or inspect server-side logs.
- The data-side container has **no internet**. Pipeline code cannot reach
  MLflow, S3, an external database, or any other outbound endpoint. If a run
  fails with a connection-shaped error, this is why — the tracker has to run
  inside the perimeter, not outside it. Where a tracking server is provided, it
  sits on a network this container is deliberately not attached to.
- Anything a node writes must stay under `data/` (enforced by both the
  `ConfineWritesToData` catalog lint and the read-only container filesystem).
- Available shell tooling is limited to `find`, `grep`, `git`, and the file I/O
  tools (`Read`, `Edit`, `Write`).

